-- title:
--   A block of integer side contains at least that many lattice points per dimension
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathbb{Z}^d \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be the integer lattice and, for a side length $\ell$ and a point $x$, let
--   $$Q_\ell(x) \;=\; \mathbb{Z}^d \cap \overline{A}_\ell(x)
--   \;=\; \bigl\{w \in \mathbb{Z}^d : \|w - x\|_\infty \le \tfrac{\ell}{2}\bigr\}$$
--   be the *block* of side $\ell$ centred at $x$, where $\|\cdot\|_\infty$ is the maximum norm. If $\ell \in \mathbb{N}$ and the centre $c$ is itself a lattice point, $c \in \mathbb{Z}^d$, then
--   $$\ell^{\,d} \;\le\; \#\,Q_\ell(c),$$
--   where $\#$ denotes the cardinality of the set, taken in $\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$.
--
--   This is the lower bound on the population of a block used in counting arguments: a block of integer side $\ell$ about a lattice site contains at least $\ell$ admissible values in each coordinate — exactly $\ell+1$ when $\ell$ is even and $\ell$ when $\ell$ is odd — so at least $\ell^d$ points in total. It is what makes a block's cardinality comparable to its volume, and hence what turns a density statement into a counting statement.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The side length is a natural number $\ell$ cast into $\mathbb{R}$ for the definition of the cube, while $\ell^d$ on the left is computed in $\mathbb{N}$ and then cast into `ℕ∞`; the cardinality is `Set.encard`, valued in `ℕ∞`, so the statement is meaningful without knowing in advance that the block is finite.
